{
  "id": "dativ",
  "name": "Verbs with two objects",
  "level": "A2",
  "status": "entwurf",
  "subtitle": "Some verbs have two objects: give, send, show, tell, buy, lend and bring. Put the person first: Give me the book. Or put the thing first and add to or for: Give the book to me. Buy a present for her.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "Give me the book",
    "Give the book to me",
    "Buy a present for her"
  ],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-a2 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "give · send · show",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "me · him · Tom"
      },
      {
        "text": "the book"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "She <span class=\"vp-verb\">gave</span> me the book. — She <span class=\"vp-verb\">gave</span> the book <span class=\"vp-prep\">to</span> me. — He <span class=\"vp-verb\">bought</span> a present <span class=\"vp-prep\">for</span> her.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>verb + person + thing</strong>. The person comes first, with no <em>to</em> or <em>for</em>: <em>Give me the book. She sent Tom an email. I'll show you the photos.</em>",
        "tag": "Person first"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>verb + thing + to + person</strong>. This is possible with <em>give, send, show, tell, lend, pass, bring, offer</em>: <em>Give the book to me. She sent an email to Tom. Pass the salt to Amira.</em>",
        "tag": "Thing first: to"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>buy, make, cook, get, find</strong> use <strong>for</strong>: <em>I bought a present for her. Dad made a cake for us.</em> The person-first pattern works too: <em>I bought her a present.</em>",
        "tag": "Thing first: for"
      },
      {
        "html": "When the thing is a pronoun (<em>it, them</em>), say it first and use <strong>to</strong> or <strong>for</strong>: <em>Give it to me. She sent them to Tom. I bought it for her.</em>",
        "tag": "it, them"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>Give to me the book.</em> → ✓ <em>Give me the book.</em> or <em>Give the book to me.</em> · ✗ <em>Give me it.</em> → ✓ <em>Give it to me.</em> · ✗ <em>She explained me the problem.</em> → ✓ <em>She explained the problem to me.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "The words for the person are the object pronouns <em>me, you, him, her, us, them</em>: see <a href=\"/akkusativ\">Object pronouns</a>. Some verbs, such as <em>explain, say</em> and <em>suggest</em>, never put the person first: <em>explain the rule to me, say hello to Tom</em>. You can use the patterns in polite questions too: <em>Can you give me a hand?</em> (see <a href=\"/modalverben\">Modal verbs</a>).",
